@heading 
Borders 
@description 
Border plantings are massed shrubs of varying shape, size and 
~texture~, but are not continues as a ~hedge~ is. 
@content 
     Border plantings may be used as the "walls" of a lot, and as
a background for ~flower beds~. Shrubs can be selected for their
appeal to ~birds~, for flowers and/or foliage.  Shrub borders are
more complicated to design than other types of plantings.  Four 
steps are involved: 
     1.  Consider the form of individual plants as rectangles and
     squares.  Place the forms in relation to each other in a 
     pleasing arrangement.  An easy way to do this is to cut out
     a variety of forms and mix and match them until you decide 
     which you prefer.  The border should be asymmetrical, with 
     height on one side of a vertical line, the tallest tree or 
     shrub, and breadth on the other.  Evaluate your composition
     and check it against the ~principles of design~ and your 
     ~site plan~. 
     2.  Now begin to think of these abstract shapes as shrub and
     tree forms.  The rectangles will become rounded, pyramidal,
     cylindrical, etc.  Do a vertical plan and then a top view as
     you would see in an architectual drawing.  You should now be
     able to see what the border will look like; make changes as
     necessary. 
     3.  Place tracing paper over the final border plan and 
     indicate ~foliage texture~ by letters.  Keep the texture 
     change graduale from coarse to medium to fine.  Use another
     sheet of tracing paper to analyse ~foliage color~, again 
     using letters.  When you have a good color selection combine
     it with the texture study.   
     4.  Match the height and spread of each plant with the 
     border plan; list plants that meet thes requirements.  Make
     the final decisions at the nursey. ~Nelson~, p.101-106.